    "image_with_text_NjQ3EM": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_ENV8fL": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Q:</strong> Your work pulls in pop-culture objects. What makes something feel right to include?</p><p><strong>A:</strong> I’m drawn to objects which are instantly recognizable, things people already have seen, like familiar packaging or branding. What makes something feel right is when I can shift the meaning through humour, language and material. I often rework these objects to reflect new perspectives, like renaming them or editing the ingredients. I look for designs and colours which I want to redesign with underglazes. </p>",
            "text_style": "body"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text_ENV8fL"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/photo-137.jpg",
        "height": "adapt",
        "desktop_image_width": "medium",
        "layout": "image_first",
        "desktop_content_position": "middle",
        "desktop_content_alignment": "left",
        "content_layout": "no-overlap",
        "color_scheme": "scheme-1",
        "mobile_content_alignment": "left",
        "padding_top": 0,
        "padding_bottom": 36
      }
    },
    "image_with_text_irtNLW": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_ppjw9W": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Q: </strong>You throw on the wheel and hand build to create your pieces. How do you decide between the two?</p><p><strong>A:</strong> It usually comes down to the collection I’m working on. I wheel-throw all my closed forms and larger pieces, while I handbuild my pop-culture product collection. Handbuilding is more intuitive and you can see my hand in the work, especially when I underglaze the pieces. My closed forms incorporate rattles inside, for a sound element, which range in size, and it's quicker to build those styles on the wheel. </p>",
            "text_style": "body"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text_ppjw9W"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/photo-121_copy.jpg",
        "height": "adapt",
        "desktop_image_width": "medium",
        "layout": "text_first",
        "desktop_content_position": "middle",
        "desktop_content_alignment": "left",
        "content_layout": "no-overlap",
        "color_scheme": "scheme-1",
        "mobile_content_alignment": "left",
        "padding_top": 0,
        "padding_bottom": 36
      }
    },
    "image_with_text_FAfVaW": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_tfc8jA": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Q:</strong> What’s currently on your studio playlist?</p><p><strong>A:</strong> Top 5 lately!</p><ol><li>Tame Impala - Let it Happen</li><li>Com Truise - Compress-Fuss</li><li>upper class - Rave Memories</li><li>Onra - Moonlit Street</li><li>James Blake - Rest Of Your Life</li></ol><p></p><p><strong>Q:</strong> How would you describe your personal style?</p><p><strong>A:</strong> Lately since I often go from the studio, to class, or work to everything else, I’d say my style is practical but thoughtful. I gravitate towards class shapes, but I care a lot about the details - fit, texture, materials. I like looking polished but not overdone. </p>",
            "text_style": "body"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text_tfc8jA"
      ],
